ambassador Bolton thank you very much  for speaking to us next week president  Trump is due to take part in a UK state  visit Theresa May of course will be  leaving office just days later why do  you think she went wrong with her  leadership well that's not a question I  think it's appropriate for me to get  into the president admires Prime  Minister May he's said that on a number  of occasions he admires her  determination and her grit he's tried to  work very closely with her because the  US UK relationship is the most important  bilateral relationship we have and I  think the two of them will have a lot to  discuss next week even though she's  announced her intention to leave the  prime ministership I think we have a lot  of issues in front of us and it's an  important celebration of the 75th  anniversary of d-day and I think the two  of them will have a lot to talk about  of course the brexit negotiations will  be handled by her successor do you think  a No Deal is the best option for Britain  now well I think president Trump's made  it clear he looks forward to the day  when the US and the UK can negotiate a  bilateral trade deal that would be  beneficial to both countries and and to  see the will of the British people  carried out now beyond that I think it's  going to be up to the Conservative Party  in the first instance to pick a new  leader and then to see what what happens  in the negotiation process with the  European Union what kind of leader would  you like to work with in the future we  know that President Trump has once again  today praised Boris Johnson and Nigel  Farage what qualities do you admire in  Boris Johnson well look the president  the president has relationships with  with both of those men as he has with  many people in Britain because of his  long-standing business interest here  he's been a strong supporter of brexit  and I think he really does look forward  to the opportunity to negotiate with a  fully independent Britain outside the  European Union but beyond that I think  at this stage it's obviously a delicate  moment in the leadership selection  process and I'll leave it at that  of course there are US commercial  interests involved in UK US trade deal  that would open up our trade our  medicine of course pharmaceutical  industry as well it has big implications  where America could gain and it has big  implications where Britain could gain  since many of these same pharmaceutical  companies do are based in Great Britain  and do a lot of business in the United  States I just think the two economies  are very much in sync I could imagine  bringing Canada into a three-way deal in  the near future Britain is a global  economic power a global political and  military power and we look forward to a  continued very strong partnership with  the UK there are protests planned next  week here during the state visit the  president is a man who cares about  optics is he concerned about those no  look I think people are going to protest  for one reason or another that's what  free societies are all about and I think  it's the it's a tribute to the strength  of the democratic system in Great  Britain and the United States that these  people are free to protest it's been a  very important few weeks for u.s.  foreign policy  you have pushed for regime change in  Iran you said the North Korea violated  UN resolutions with the recent missile  tests and yet the president said quite  the opposite so who's right well you  know on Iran the president's policy is  not regime change it never has been I've  said many many things over the years  before I took this job on a wide variety  of subjects I've hardly hidden my views  under a bushel basket but in this job  I'm the national security adviser I'm  not the national security decision-maker  and obviously the president dictates the  policy and that's certainly true in  North Korea as well the president is  very determined that neither Iran nor  North Korea will get deliverable nuclear  weapons he has been fully prepared to  negotiate with either of both countries  he said so since the 2016 campaign he's  held two summit meetings with Kim  jong-un a press  than breaking a pair of summits and he's  held fast to the position he's expressed  there that he wants to see all of North  Korea's nuclear weapons program  eliminated so I think his position is  very clear and that's obviously the the  position of the administration your  position on Iranian naval vessels you  say were to blame for damage to oil  tankers in the Gulf of Oman you said  that was an act of aggression but not  one the US was going to respond to so  what would it take for America to  respond well I've said it was almost  certainly the case that Iran was  responsible for the attack on the  tankers we expect that the countries of  the vessels involved Saudi Arabia the  United Arab Emirates Norway will in due  course and I think fairly shortly make  public the results of their  investigations I don't think anybody who  knows the region has any doubt who  staged those attacks or the attacks on  the Saudi pipeline pumping stations  whether Iran or their surrogates we've  made it very clear that this kind of  behavior is not something they should be  engaged in and we've made it  particularly clear that if American  citizens or facilities are threatened or  attacked that there will be a very  strong response back in Washington  Democrats are now pushing for  impeachment proceedings after Robert  Muller said that he wasn't able to  determine whether or not the president  had committed a crime are you preparing  for that eventualities well look I'm not  preparing for it because it's not in my  area responsibility I think the  president's made his position on this  very clear and the Justice Department  and Attorney General bar have made their  position on it clear and I think we'll  see what happens in Congress it's the  political season the 2020 campaign is on  us so fasten your seatbelt President  Trump says it's filthy disgusting word  impeachment do you agree well I think  it's obviously something the Democrats  have to think long and hard about before  they go down this route I don't think  the framers of the Constitution ever  intended impeachment to be  a political process I think they had  something very different in mind  and it's something that having watched  Republicans tried against President  Clinton during his administration and  see it backfire something I think  politicians should reflect on there's  been a lot of discussion in the United  States about you as a hawk in the White  House and Donald Trump as the Dove do  you ever feel isolated  you know whenever these stories appear  in the press I think back to a an old  proverb from Central Asia that goes the  dog's bark and the caravan moves on  you've previously said that when you  enter government you have to accept that  there are things that you're not going  to win are you winning or losing at the  moment I'm having a great time you won't  be drawn on either side nice try and the  state visit what do you hope it will  achieve between the two countries well I  think it will underline the strength of  the bilateral relationship I think it  will make it very clear that the  interest and values of the United  Kingdom in the United States are very  very close regardless of political  currents in either country I think this  is a time of challenge for free  societies around the world and I think  it's very important that everyone  understand just how close our two  countries are it's been a very  problematic period in British political  history a lot of criticism of Teresa  Mays leadership who do you think you'll  see in Downing Street that's a  prediction someone else will have to  make those obviously though a lot of  contact with various members of the  British political establishment we know  that Nigel Farage has been to Washington  that's talk of him agitated f